---
import SourceList from './SourceList.astro';
import type { Source } from '../data/catalog';
import { pollPath } from '../data/polling';

interface Props { slug: 'fpoll' | 'fselect' | 'fcntl' }
const { slug } = Astro.props;
const control = slug === 'fcntl';
const sources: Source[] = [
  ['ptos', 'bdos/bdosmain.c', 'GEMDOS dispatch'],
  ...(control ? [
    ['ptos', 'bdos/fsclose.c', 'Descriptor lookup and F_GETFL behavior'],
    ['ptos', 'doc/open-file-description-audit.md', 'Open-file description and close semantics'],
  ] satisfies Source[] : [
    ['ptos', 'doc/fpoll-time64.txt', 'Legacy polling behavior and build requirements'],
    ['libcmini', 'include/mint/mintbind.h', '68k macros and ARM and x32 declarations'],
    ['libcmini', 'sources/fpoll_time64.c', 'libcmini compatibility adapters'],
    ['libcmini', 'tests/fpoll_time64/test.c', 'Legacy timeout and bitmap regression tests'],
  ] satisfies Source[]),
  ['paratos', 'host/posix/file.c', 'ParaTOS POSIX file operations'],
  ['paratos', 'host/ptos/dispatch.c', 'ParaTOS pTOS-hosted GEMDOS dispatch'],
];
---
<section aria-labelledby="ptos-file-notes">
  <h2 id="ptos-file-notes" class="prm-section-heading ruled">pTOS behavior</h2>
  {control ? <>
    <p>Native pTOS builds with <code>CONF_WITH_USER_ASPACE</code> implement <code>Fcntl(handle, 0L, F_GETFL)</code>
      with command value <code>3</code>. The <code>arg</code> slot is ignored. The result is the open access mode
      <code>0</code> for read-only, <code>1</code> for write-only or <code>2</code> for read/write. BIOS device handles
      report <code>2</code>. Invalid descriptors return a negative lookup error such as <code>EIHNDL</code>.</p>
    <p>Other commands return <code>EINVFN</code>. The internal PFS <code>ioctl</code> callback is a kernel interface.
      For native readiness, use <a href={pollPath}>Fpoll_time64</a>.</p>
  </> : <>
    {slug === 'fpoll' ? <>
      <p><code>Fpoll</code> uses the eight-byte <code>POLLFD</code> record and a signed 32-bit timeout in milliseconds.
        Zero polls immediately. Every negative value means an indefinite wait. The return value counts entries with a
        nonzero <code>revents</code> field. Use <a href={pollPath}>Fpoll_time64</a> for the record, event flags and a 64-bit timeout.</p>
      <pre class="prm-code"><code>long Fpoll(void *fds, unsigned long nfds, long timeout_ms);</code></pre>
    </> : <>
      <p><code>Fselect</code> accepts an unsigned 16-bit timeout in milliseconds. Zero waits indefinitely. Each non-null
        mask points to a 32-bit input/output bitmap for descriptors 0–31. The fourth argument can select exceptional
        readiness. Errors or hangups set the bit in every requested mask, including an exception-only wait.</p>
      <p>The result counts ready bits across the supplied masks before any output masks are combined. Masks that share
        an address receive the bitwise union of their results. An invalid selected descriptor clears the masks and returns
        <code>EIHNDL</code>. See <a href={pollPath}>Fpoll_time64</a> for the underlying readiness semantics.</p>
      <pre class="prm-code"><code>int Fselect(unsigned short timeout, void *read_mask, void *write_mask, void *except_mask);</code></pre>
    </>}
    <p>These C declarations describe the ARM and x32 libcmini adapters. They call the native <code>0x194</code> trap.
      On 68k, libcmini uses the original <code>TRAP #1</code> opcode shown on this page. Both paths require
      <code>CONF_WITH_FPOLL_TIME64</code> and a supported managed caller.</p>
  </>}
  <p>ParaTOS supports this legacy call through its POSIX backend. The pTOS-hosted guest dispatcher leaves it unimplemented.</p>
  <SourceList {sources} />
</section>
